==============================================================================
T1. RANK OF THE COORDINATE MAP phi(H, p, L) = (d, q)
==============================================================================
  mixed, H > 0 > L         rank {2: 400}   (400 random points)
  no loss branch, L > 0    rank {1: 400}   (400 random points)
  no gain branch, H < 0    rank {1: 400}   (400 random points)

  rank two on the mixed region and rank one on the unmixed regions is
  the precise statement that the coordinate degenerates there.

==============================================================================
T2. IS d DIFFERENTIABLE ACROSS L = 0 ?
==============================================================================
  analytic one-sided derivatives at L = 0:
    from the mixed side   dd/dL = 2(1-p)/(pH)
    from the gain side    dd/dL = 0

     H      p     numeric from below   analytic   from above
    50.0   0.50           0.040000   0.040000    0.000000
    20.0   0.80           0.025000   0.025000    0.000000
    80.0   0.25           0.075000   0.075000    0.000000
    10.0   0.60           0.133333   0.133333    0.000000

  d is continuous and NOT differentiable at L = 0. The derivative
  from the mixed side is bounded away from zero and the derivative
  from the other side is exactly zero.

==============================================================================
T3. FRONTIER CONDITION FOR THE GRAPH OF kappa
==============================================================================
  Whitney A and B presuppose that the closure of the big stratum
  contains the small one. For a graph that is an empirical question.

    stratum                n      kappa       se
    inside_0.00_0.02       768    +0.7091   0.1594
    inside_0.02_0.05      1266    +0.6653   0.1390
    inside_0.05_0.10      1736    +0.3588   0.1285
    inside_0.10_0.20      3584    -0.1002   0.0796
    edge                 35617    -0.1530   0.0253

    limit from inside  +0.7091
    value on the edge  -0.1530
    difference         -0.8621 +/- 0.1614   (5.3 se)

    FRONTIER CONDITION: FAILS. kappa does not extend continuously to the edge, so the
    graph is not a stratified set and Whitney A and B are
    INAPPLICABLE to it, not merely unverified.

==============================================================================
T4. WHITNEY A FOR THE GRAPH, IN THE q DIRECTION
==============================================================================
  if the frontier condition held, A would require the q slope of
  kappa just inside to converge to the q slope on the edge.

    stratum                   n     kappa_0      q slope
    inside 0.00 to 0.02         768    +3.6660    -3.0986 +/- 2.9959
    inside 0.02 to 0.05        1266    +0.6652    -0.0196 +/- 0.1639
    inside 0.05 to 0.10        1736    +0.3007    +0.3155 +/- 0.1840
    inside 0.10 to 0.20        3584    -0.0450    +0.1285 +/- 0.1543
    edge                     35617    -0.0299    +0.3896 +/- 0.0406

    innermost minus edge  -3.4882 +/- 2.9961   (1.2 se)
    WHITNEY A in q: consistent

==============================================================================
T5. A SECOND SEAM. q JUMPS WHERE |H| = |L|
==============================================================================
  q = 2 p_salient - 1 and the salient outcome is the larger in
  absolute value, so at |H| = |L| the definition switches and q
  jumps by 2|2p - 1|.

    |L| clearly larger   n  10663   mean q -0.346   mean jump if switched 0.979
    |L| just larger      n   2082   mean q -0.074   mean jump if switched 0.867
    |H| just larger      n   1319   mean q +0.218   mean jump if switched 0.737
    |H| clearly larger   n  41921   mean q -0.220   mean jump if switched 0.906

    rows within 12 percent of |H| = |L|: 3401

